Subject: canvas: the persona's text sits at its own sub-pixel phase, at raster time

Two profiles launched from one machine draw one canvas, because the bytes come
out of this host's Skia and this host's driver and the catalogue has no second
rasteriser to offer. Patch 0120 answered that at the readback boundary, behind
`--fingerprint-noise`, and measurement says that boundary is the wrong place to
stand. This patch moves the perturbation to where the pixels are made.

WHAT THE READBACK PLACEMENT COSTS, MEASURED

Through the shipped `macos-arm64` binary, seed 42 windows, one page served over
the loopback so a worker has an origin, `--fingerprint-noise` on and off:

  export to a data URL, decode, draw, read, four generations
    off  all four byte-identical
    on   every generation differs from the last, every differing byte by
         exactly one, two generations apart by two

  a 256px linear gradient from #000000 to #ffffff, read back and compared
  against the ramp the caller asked for
    off  deviation in [-1, 0], monotone, 0 of 255 steps go backwards
    on   deviation in [-2, +1], 32 of 255 steps go backwards

  toDataURL against getImageData on one canvas
    off  identical
    on   28428 bytes differ

The first two need no reference sample and no statistics. A page exports its
own canvas, imports it and exports it again, and a real browser hands back the
bytes it was given; this one drifts, because each egress route applies the
policy once more and the routes compose. A page draws a ramp it specified
analytically and checks that it never goes backwards, and hardware cannot
produce a gradient that does; this one does, 32 times, because a step of one on
a value near a rounding boundary is a step against the ramp.

Both are properties of the placement rather than of the arithmetic. Any policy
applied at the moment bytes leave the surface is applied again when those bytes
come back and leave a second time, and any policy that moves a pixel the caller
computed analytically is visible against the analytic value. Making the step
idempotent would close the first and not the second, and would still leave the
route table -- getImageData, toDataURL, toBlob, readPixels, the PIXEL_PACK
path, ImageBitmap -- as a list that has to stay complete as Chromium adds
routes.

WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD

The canvas text origin gets a sub-pixel offset drawn from the profile identity,
applied once, before the draw. Nothing downstream is hooked. The perturbed
pixels are the canvas, so every consumption route -- the two 2D readbacks, a
`drawImage` copy, a WebGL `texImage2D` upload, an OffscreenCanvas in a worker,
and whatever route Chromium adds next -- reads the same surface, and there is
no second application to compose with the first.

The offset is `[-0.25, 0.25]` device-independent pixels on x, in 1/64 steps, 33
values. It is not applied to y: Skia takes sub-pixel positions on x and rounds
y, so a fractional y is either inert or a whole-pixel baseline jump, and a
baseline a pixel off its metrics is a difference no machine has.

A quarter pixel is the bound because that is the span the claimed platforms
already disagree over. After patch 0123 all three personas render text with
`SkFont::setSubpixel(true)` -- Windows through `font_platform_data_win.cc:67`
whenever the text is antialiased, macOS hard-coded, Linux through
`web_font_render_style.cc:106` for any hinting short of full -- so each glyph
lands in one of Skia's quarter-pixel bins, and which bin depends on where the
run started. Shifting the run start inside a quarter pixel moves a subset of
the glyphs into the neighbouring bin and leaves the rest, which is the same
coverage difference two machines produce when their hinting disagrees. Every
frame this draws is a frame the claimed platform draws for some origin.

WHY THIS IS NOT A SWITCH

It is on wherever a profile is on. `--fingerprint=host` and a launch with no
profile keep the host's own phase, because there is no identity to key on and a
host-derived offset would be a host fact in the pixels. An authored profile
with no `source_id` likewise keeps it.

It costs nothing a page can price. `measureText` is computed from font metrics
and is not touched; the damage rectangle and the rendered-text capture both
read `location` after the offset, so bounds follow the ink. What it does not do
is what 0120 did: a canvas that draws no text is identical across profiles on
one host, and so is a WebGL readback. That is the honest ceiling of doing this
at raster time, and the reason `--fingerprint-noise` stays in the tree, off by
default, with what it costs now measured in docs/LIMITATIONS.md.

DETERMINISM

`PersistentHash` over a NUL-separated domain and the profile id, the
`SyntheticDeviceSeed` construction from `base/apostate/media_devices.cc`, and
the same one 0120 uses under its own domain string. Stable across builds,
processes and launches, so a persistent profile keeps its phase and a seed
reproduces it on another host. Resolved once per process and read from a
function-local static; no clock, no counter, no `base::Rand*`.

Verification: V0 and V1 here, with `CanvasTextPhaseTest` covering the empty-id
and no-profile cases, the range bound, the step quantum and stability across
two resolutions of one id. V3 is the four generations and the ramp above, which
this patch turns into the noise-off column.

